from ./Cli import Cli
from ./Config import Config
from ./Errors import Errors
from ./Formatting import Formatting
from ./OutputSize import OutputSize

export anchor Init:
    description:
        `emul init` starts a new Emul project, as described in @{Cli}. It
        writes a config file and an entry file that build straight away, so
        `emul build` and `emul preview` work the moment it finishes.

    directory:
        `emul init` takes an optional directory to create the project in.
        Without one, it uses the current directory. A directory that does not
        exist yet is created, along with any missing parents.

        ```text
        \\\
        emul init
        emul init my-designs
        emul init my-designs --name Marketing --width 375 --format png
        \\\
        ```

    files:
        - emul.config.pi
            The project's config, as described in @{Config}. It sets the
            entry, outDir, formats, width and height explicitly, so the basic
            settings are visible and ready to change. The background and
            svgFonts settings are written commented out, with their defaults,
            so they are easy to find. Settings given by flags are written with
            the flag's value in place of the default.
        - index.emul
            The entry file. It declares a few small components and exports
            one composition that uses them, so the first build renders
            something. The composition is named after the project, with
            `Design` appended if that would clash with a starter component's
            name.
        - .gitignore
            Ignores the output directory. It is written only when the
            directory has no `.gitignore`; an existing one is left alone.

    formatted:
        Every file `emul init` writes is already in its canonical layout, so
        `emul format --check` passes on a new project, as described in
        @{Formatting}. A new project also passes `emul check` with no errors
        and no warnings.

    name:
        The project name is used for the `emul-config` anchor and the starter
        composition. It is the directory's name in PascalCase, so
        `my-designs` becomes `MyDesigns`. When that is not a valid name, such
        as for a directory whose name starts with a digit, it is `Project`.

    flags:
        - name
            `--name <Name>` sets the project name. It must be PascalCase: a
            capital letter, then letters and digits.
        - width
            `--width <px>` sets the width setting. Defaults to `1280`.
        - height
            `--height <px>` or `--height auto` sets the height setting.
            Defaults to `auto`.
        - background
            `--background <color>` sets the background setting, which is then
            written uncommented.
        - format
            `--format <format>` sets the formats setting. It may be repeated.
            Defaults to all of `html`, `svg` and `png`.
        - outDir
            `--out <dir>` sets the outDir setting. Defaults to `./out`.
        - force
            `--force` overwrites an existing `emul.config.pi` or
            `index.emul`.

        Width, height and background are checked the same way as the
        matching flags of `emul build`, described in @{OutputSize}.

    existingProject:
        Without `--force`, `emul init` changes nothing when the directory
        already has an `emul.config.pi` or an `index.emul`. It reports each
        one with the code `project-exists` and exits with code 1, as
        described in @{Errors}. Other files already in the directory, such as
        other `.emul` files, are never touched.

    output:
        Each file written is reported as `created <path>`, then a short note
        on what to run next.

        ```text
        \\\
        created my-designs/emul.config.pi
        created my-designs/index.emul
        created my-designs/.gitignore

        next:
          cd my-designs
          emul preview
        \\\
        ```

        The `cd` line is left out when the project is in the current
        directory.
